Licensing
=========

This project is Apache-2.0 for the product and the end-to-end harness, and
MIT OR Apache-2.0 for the tooling around them. The split is not a matter of
taste: it follows what each file's own `SPDX-License-Identifier:` header
says, and the audit in `tests/license.rs` checks every source file against
the claim made here.

`LICENSE` at the root is the canonical, machine-readable declaration: the
verbatim Apache-2.0 text, the one GitHub's licence detector reads. This file
is the human-readable map behind it. Because the tooling's `MIT OR
Apache-2.0` always includes Apache-2.0, the whole repository is available
under Apache-2.0; the MIT option is an additional permission on those four
trees, not a different licence for the shipped binaries. Every package
manifest in this repository and its distribution repos states
`Apache-2.0` for that reason.

Apache-2.0 only
----------------

The product. Every source file in these trees carries
`SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0` on its first line, and the crate
manifests state the same terms. The three files that once came from
`fsearch` under MIT -- the query engine, the name index and the content
search layer -- were each replaced by an independent Apache-2.0
implementation; no third-party source file remains in the product.
`tests/license.rs` keeps an explicit (now empty) exception list so a
future file claiming another licence has to be named on purpose.

    crates/name-core/                  the name index, the query engine, the fuzzy ranker
    crates/par/                        the parallelism: split the work, run the pieces
    crates/discovery/                  the discovery seam
    crates/discovery-windows/          MFT + USN discovery
    crates/discovery-linux/            getdents64 walk
    crates/discovery-macos/            FSEvents + getattrlistbulk
    crates/discovery-freebsd/          the FreeBSD walk
    crates/discovery-dispatch/         the per-OS chooser
    crates/engine/                     orchestration: keep-warm, TV pack store glue
    crates/proto/                      the daemon protocol
    crates/autostart/                  the login-item registration (parity row 4.13)
    crates/launcher/                   the launcher's model: query, results, actions, geometry
    crates/launcher-windows/           the launcher's window on Windows: Win32 + GDI
    crates/launcher-x11/               the launcher's window on X11 (Linux, FreeBSD): overlay + XEmbed tray
    crates/pick/                       the hand-off to an external picker (fzf and its family)
    crates/adapters/                   the rgx adapter registry: the formats a search reads into
    bins/searchctl/                   the CLI
    bins/searchd/                      the daemon
    bins/search-ui/                    the terminal UI
    bins/zen-rs/                       the zen CLI (NTFS indexer)
    bins/rgxy/                         the launcher over the daemon
    tests/                             the end-to-end harness over the user-facing bins
    .winget/                           the winget package manifests (parity row 4.15)
    Formula/                           the Homebrew formula (parity row 4.15)

The two packaging trees carry the same header as everything else, with one
exception worth stating: the winget manifests put it on the line *after* the
`yaml-language-server` schema comment, because that comment has to be the
first line for the schema to be picked up. The formula carries it on the
first line like the rest.

The vendored rgx crates are Apache-2.0 as well, and for the same reason
they are Apache-2.0 everywhere: they are upstream rgx's own files,
transported verbatim, and upstream offers Apache-2.0 and no other option.
This project does not relicense somebody else's files.

    crates/rgx-core/                   the content engine, the codecs, the pack store
    crates/rar-codec/                  the RAR decoder
    crates/rgx-adapters-archive/       archives: 7z, tar, zip, rar, ar, lz4, ...
    crates/rgx-adapters-docs/          soffice and tesseract adapters
    crates/rgx-adapters-inspect/       PE, ELF, Mach-O, x509, CAD, CRAM, ...
    crates/rgx-adapters-llm/           gguf, safetensors, npy, protobuf, ...
    crates/rgx-adapters-mail/          mbox, eml, msg, pst, ...
    crates/rgx-adapters-media/         EXIF/TIFF, avi, asf, flv, mpeg, ...
    crates/rgx-adapters-office/        docx, pptx, xlsx, rtf, tex, visio, ...
    crates/rgx-adapters-pdf/           the PDF reader, JBIG2, fax, security
    crates/rgx-adapters-tabular/       dbf, mdb, hdf5, netcdf, zarr, ...
    crates/rgx-adapters-threed/        glb, stl, ply, fbx, blend, nifti, ...

Five of those files encode a file *layout* that was learned from somebody
else's implementation, and upstream records where that knowledge came from
in its NOTICE. A layout is a fact about a file format rather than code,
the code that encodes it was written here, and no upstream source file is
in the tree — so no second licence composes into these files, and every
one of them carries Apache-2.0 and nothing else. `NOTICE` names them.

MIT OR Apache-2.0
-------------------

The tooling. You may use this code under the terms of either licence;
commercial use of it is licensed under Apache-2.0, which is the licence
this project intends for commercial deployment, and the MIT option remains
available to anyone who prefers its terms.

    scripts/                           the tooling: vendoring, drift, the protocol harness
    tools/                             helper programs
    benchmark/                         the measured tables and the write-ups behind them
    install.sh, install.ps1            the one-line installers (one per platform)

No source file in this repository carries another project's licence. The
`fsearch` files that once did were replaced; `LICENSE-MIT-FSEARCH` was
removed with the last of them. The vendored rgx trees keep upstream's
Apache-2.0 terms and their own per-crate `LICENSE-APACHE`.

Full texts: LICENSE-APACHE and LICENSE-MIT. Third-party notices: NOTICE.

Consequence for the distributed binaries
----------------------------------------

Every binary this project ships is Apache-2.0: the product is Apache-2.0,
the end-to-end harness is Apache-2.0, and the vendored rgx crates are
Apache-2.0 too. The toolkit is the exception: `scripts/`, `tools/`,
`benchmark/` and the two installers are MIT OR Apache-2.0, and a
distribution that includes them is governed by Apache-2.0 for that part.
